# The builder stage does `COPY . /app`, so without this file the whole working tree ends
# up in an image layer - including the developer's real `src/.env`. That is not just
# untidy: `SECRET_KEY`, `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` and `ADMIN_PASSWORD` become part of a
# distributable artifact, recoverable from the layer regardless of what later stages copy.
#
# Note that .gitignore does NOT apply to Docker build context - a file can be correctly
# untracked by git and still be baked into every image.

# ---- secrets ----
# `.env.example` is deliberately not matched by this and is still copied.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# ---- version control / tooling ----
.git
.gitignore
.github
.dockerignore

# ---- local virtualenv and caches ----
.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
.pytest_cache
.coverage
htmlcov

# ---- runtime artefacts that must never ship ----
# `src/app/logs/app.log` was being copied into the runtime image. With `SMTP_HOST`
# unset - the documented local-development setup - that file contains magic-link URLs
# with live sign-in tokens in them.
**/*.log
**/logs
crudadmin_data

# ---- editor / OS noise ----
**/.DS_Store
.idea
.vscode

# ---- docs and build-time tooling: not needed at runtime ----
docs
*.md
!README.md
# The deploy bundle describes how to *run* the published image; it has no business inside
# one.
deploy
# Regenerates `src/app/data/`, by hand and over the network. The generated file ships; the
# generator has no business in a runtime image.
scripts

# Claude Code working directory - agent worktrees, easily hundreds of MB, and
# every byte would be uploaded as part of the build context.
.claude/
